WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.120
Good Morning, John.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.220
What is going on here? 
This is a Dr. Pepper can.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.020
And it's never been opened, 
and it is doing this.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.740
And it feels like it's super thin, 
like it's a weird aluminum.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.160
But it's only because aluminum cans are actually, 
they're very fragile.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.920
Except that they have high tensile strength so when they're being inflated

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.000
by the pressure inside of them, 
they actually get quite strong.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.320
When they're not, they're weak and floppy 
but this has never leaked.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.060
It's never been opened, but it's not got pressure in it.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.260
So, let's open the normal one.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.600
So, that sounded normal.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.360
Now, let's open this. My guess is-

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.640
it's gonna sound different.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.160
Yeah. This is a flat -

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.200
Dr. Pepper. 
What's happening?

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.040
Normal Dr. Pepper, it tastes like regular Dr. Pepper.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.220
And then this -

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.860
- barest tiniest tingle. 
Authentic Blend of 23 Flavors!

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.600
But a - You missed one!

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.720
So I guess, the carbonation is necessary 
to make a coke can feel like a coke can.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.000
Otherwise, when it gets cold 
all of the gas inside contracts

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.220
and then you get a weird deflated coke can.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.440
I guess.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.620
If anybody has any better theories than that, 
let me know.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.980
John, I've been thinking a lot about stories recently

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.940
Ah, because I've been working on my own, because I've just read yours,

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.940
and because it's the day after the 30th anniversary of Star Trek: The Next Generation.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.480
The most formative piece of media in my life.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.340
So, there's a guy who I never thought about 
when I was a young person.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.060
Didn't know he existed but 
who had a tremendous impact on me.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.280
Michael Piller was the showrunner for
Star Trek: The Next Generation.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.320
From the third season to the end of the show.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.020
The last couple years he shared that duty 
with Jeri Taylor.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.620
These people wrote a lot of episodes of Star Trek 
but also managed the writing room.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.200
Show running is a thing that back then,
in the 90s wasn't -

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.600
-  thought about by normal people, certainly not by me.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:43.580
I was like eleven.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.380
I was just watching an interview with Michael Piller today and he was -

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.600
- saying that, uh, they once went in with an idea

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.860
for a show and talked to Gene Roddenberry
 the creator of Star Trek about (it).

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.700
And the episode was like an adventurous chase across the universe.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.260
And Gene said, "What's it about?"

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.200
And they were like, "Well it's about an 
adventurous chase across the universe."

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.600
But the thing about
Star Trek: The Next Generation,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.460
the thing that I don't find too often these days in TV Sci-fi -

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.920
- is that it was always about something.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.260
Like it was about the plot, but I feel like 
if they were gonna sacrifice plot or meaning,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.400
they were gonna sacrifice plot first.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:21.280
It's so easy to write a story about good versus evil and spaceships blowing up,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.740
it's hard to write a story about disability 
or racism or what it means to be human

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.960
or like super cheesy stuff like 
the value of honesty, right?

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.320
That's a hard thing to write about these days.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.120
Like we're no longer The West Wing World,
we are The House of Cards World.

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.860
I'm a little- I'm a little bit sick of it and I think it's kind of boring.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.880
I want to know what's it about.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.620
And if you tell me it's about how messed up the world is and just evil people doing evil things-

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:45.660
I'm done!

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.780
I'm done with it. I don't want it anymore.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.100
Give me stories that are about something. And something that I can be hopeful about.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.380
Maybe? I mean -

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:55.440
It's hard. Right. Don't get me wrong.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.980
When people ask me what's Turtles All The Way Down about, the first thing I think about isn't the plot.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.440
Which is great. Like it's very interesting, like it kept me hooked throughout the whole book.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.280
It's the themes. It's how do we maintain relationships with people who have problems with their brains?

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.060
How do we understand people who experience the world in fundamentally different ways than we do?

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.820
How is that a thing with people's brains just working differently but also in a world with such -

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.500
dramatic inequality as we have right now?

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.640
You know nothing about what happens in the book 
from what I said but -

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.990
-  maybe you know what it's about.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:27.020
Which is a thing I feel like we're not necessarily asking our self -

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:31.340
- in today's media enough. I think I learned from 
Star Trek: The Next Generation

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:36.040
-which I hope you all will join me in watching a ton of this weekend on Netflix-

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.700
that themes are as important,
sometimes more important, than plots.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.680
'Coz diversion is kind of the least of what a story can offer us.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.520
We have to think about the stories that our world is built upon and that our selves are built upon,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.840
which is, it turns out, a great way to create media whether it's video blogs or books or TV shows.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.780
John, I'm really proud of you and your book. 
I cannot wait for other people to read it.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.780
And I'll see you on Tuesday.

